  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The two-day work-from-home policy is predicted to cut leavers over the next 12 months by about 35%, from 783 to 509 out of 5,000 (roughly 274 fewer people leaving), with a clearly positive average reaction of +1.21 on a -2 to +2 scale.",
    "most_at_risk": "Staff with long commutes (-40% leaving) and caregivers (-39%) gain the most because they get back two to three hours on each home day, while short-commute staff gain less (-28%) and short-commute managers say they will probably still come in most days anyway.",
    "risks": [
      "Managers with short commutes say they will keep coming in most days. That can quietly signal that 'real' commitment means being in the office, which would discourage their teams from using the home days and erode the retention gain.",
      "Employees are relying on the promise that reviews and promotions will not change. If people who work from home appear to fall behind in promotions, trust and much of the retention benefit could reverse quickly.",
      "The modelled reactions are uniformly positive and almost entirely about commute time. The simulation may understate practical problems such as coordination on Wednesdays and Fridays, roles that need to be on site, or Fridays being treated as a soft day off, so the 35% should be read as a ballpark figure rather than a forecast."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Ask senior leaders and managers to visibly use the Wednesday and Friday home days themselves, and tell them explicitly that office attendance on those days must not be treated as a sign of commitment.",
      "Track review scores and promotion rates by how often people work from home. Publish the result after the first review cycle to prove the 'no change' promise holds.",
      "Roll the policy out with clear expectations for home days (core hours, response times, meeting norms) and measure actual quit rates against the prior year. If possible, compare departments that start earlier with those that start later, so the real effect can be checked against the 35% prediction."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, the direction is consistent across every group and is driven by a clear, plausible reason (commute time saved), but the size of the effect rests on 48 modelled voices that are unusually uniform, so treat the 35% as directional rather than precise."
  },
